{"name":"Inchworm","hex":"#b2ec5d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"inchworm","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The larva of a moth of the family Geometridae."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move in a looping fashion, like an inchworm.","To crawl or creep slowly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/inchworm"}],"entries":[{"title":"Inchworm (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Inchworm\", also known as \"The Inch Worm\", is a song originally performed by Danny Kaye in the 1952 film Hans Christian Andersen. It was written by Frank Loesser."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inchworm_(song)","wikidata":"Q6014537"},{"title":"Inchworm (toy)","blocks":[{"text":"The Inchworm was a ride-on toy for children produced by the Hasbro Corporation, first introduced in the early 1970s. Inchworm was designed by Joseph M. Burck while he worked for Marvin Glass and Associates. Burck built the first inchworm using his clothes-dryer's hose and tested it with his then-three-year-old son. A scooter with yellow wheels in the shape of a green caterpillar wearing a yellow hat, the Inchworm was jointed under the saddle so its wheelbase could expand and contract. The wheels were constricted by a ratchet mechanism to rotating forward. As the rider bounced up and down on the saddle, the toy moved forward in a way somewhat resembling a Geometer caterpillar, with the ratcheting wheels making a clicking sound."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inchworm_(toy)","wikidata":"Q16920336"},{"title":"Geometer moth","blocks":[{"text":"The geometer moths are moths belonging to the family Geometridae of the insect order Lepidoptera, the moths and butterflies. Their scientific name derives from the Ancient Greek γεω, and μέτρον \"measure\" in reference to the way their larvae, or inchworms, appear to measure the earth as they move along in a looping fashion. Geometridae is a very large family, containing around 23,000 described species; over 1400 species from six subfamilies are indigenous to North America alone. A well-known member is the peppered moth, Biston betularia, which has been the subject of numerous studies in population genetics. Several other geometer moths are notorious pests."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Chiasma_species_W_IMG_2775.jpg/330px-Chiasma_species_W_IMG_2775.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geometer_moth","wikidata":"Q45559"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Geometridae-Dinakarr-10Jun11-DSC_0186.jpg/330px-Geometridae-Dinakarr-10Jun11-DSC_0186.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Geometridae-Dinakarr-10Jun11-DSC_0186.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Geometridae-Dinakarr-10Jun11-DSC_0186.jpg/330px-Geometridae-Dinakarr-10Jun11-DSC_0186.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeometridae-Dinakarr-10Jun11-DSC_0186.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Geometridae_locomotion.jpg/250px-Geometridae_locomotion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Geometridae_locomotion.jpg/500px-Geometridae_locomotion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Geometridae_locomotion.jpg/250px-Geometridae_locomotion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeometridae_locomotion.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb